            <ArticleTitle>Chiropractic Management of a Patient With Neck-Tongue Syndrome: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>321-324</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this case report was to describe the chiropractic management of a patient with neck-tongue syndrome (NTS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL FEATURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 34-year-old female patient sought treatment at a chiropractic clinic for symptoms involving neck pain associated with left-sided paresthesia of the tongue that had persisted for &gt;2 years. A diagnosis of NTS was made.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION AND OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patient was treated with spinal manipulation, myofascial release, and home exercises. After 2 weeks, she was symptom free. At the 2-year follow-up, the patient remained free of symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This patient with NTS responded favorably to a course of chiropractic care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
